                    // wins over the member select: Surelog leaves that pattern
                    // op untyped and `getValue`'s flatten is gated on the
                    // op having a typespec.
                    //   ...and so does a SIMPLE (positional) pattern, for the
                    // SAME reason and measured the same way.  `getValue`'s
                    // flatten runs only `if (const ref_typespec *rt =
                    // op->Typespec())`, and Surelog leaves a `'{33, 44}`
                    // elaborated op UNTYPED — grid `m3`'s `pk::SPAT`, where
                    // golden and our PRISTINE binary agree object for object
                    // (flat constant operands, NO typespec) and running the
                    // flat walk over it MINTED a typespec golden does not
                    // have.  That was measured as an AWAY-GOLDEN move on the
                    // first build of this law and is the reason for this line.
                    // The gate reads `k0` — the RESOLVED pattern kind, which
                    // for a CHAINED parameter (`localparam in_t CHN = PAT;`)
                    // is the referenced parameter's own pattern — so `m3`'s
                    // `CHN` still flattens and still carries its leftover,
                    // exactly as golden does, while `SPAT` does not.
                    // § patParamPatElem26v (r11v26) — ...and it refuses a
                    // pattern with a PATTERN-VALUED ELEMENT for the producer's
                    // own reason, the same reason `SPAT` is refused: Surelog
                    // leaves that operation UNTYPED and `getValue`'s flatten
                    // reads `op->Typespec()`.  Measured golden rows in the
                    // definition of `patParamPatElem26v`; without it `pk.UU`
                    // moved AWAY from golden on a nested-pattern element (v4,
                    // v11), a packed-array member's pattern element (v14) and
                    // an element naming a pattern-valued parameter (v10),
                    // every one of which the PRISTINE binary had right.
                    const Expression* pe0 =
                        structOvPat ? structOvPat
                        : structRefPat9 ? structRefPat9
                                        : &stripImplicitConv(*init);
                    if (structPat && inPackage && structWidePkg &&
                        !structHasSymbolicMemberDim(pctS) &&
                        k0 == ExpressionKind::StructuredAssignmentPattern &&
                        pe0 && !patParamPatElem26v(*pe0) &&
                        ps.getParentScope() &&
                        evalFuncWholeReads26(*ps.getParentScope(), ps)) {
                        structWidePkg = false;
                        structNoScopedRefPkg = false;
                    }
                    // § modDefImportPkgSelFlat60 (r11u60) — THE IMPORT-COPY
                    // FACE of § unpackedPkgPatFlat49 / § pkgScopeMemberSelect49.
                    //
                    // THE LAW.  A module's or interface's DEFINITION listing
                    // carries a MATERIALIZED COPY of every parameter it imports
                    // from a package, and golden TYPES and FLATTENS that copy —
                    // op75 -> ref_typespec -> the package's struct_typespec, one
                    // constant per member at the MEMBER WIDTH — exactly when the
                    // DECLARING PACKAGE'S OWN SCOPE member-selects the parameter.
                    //
                    // THE PRODUCER.  Surelog's `substituteAssignedValue` reduces
                    // the package's own `param_assign` IN PLACE for every
                    // `P.member` select compiled inside the package, and the
                    // module definition's import copy is a CLONE of that same
                    // `param_assign`.  So only a reduce that ALREADY RAN during
                    // the package's compilation is visible in the clone.  The
                    // SCOPED-REFERENCE trigger (`CompileHelper::getValue`'s
                    // `pkg::NAME` arm) reduces the package listing too, but it
                    // runs while a MODULE BODY is compiled — after the module
                    // definitions have been materialized — so its clone keeps
                    // the tagged tree.  That asymmetry is the whole law, and it
                    // is what grid arm `g1` isolates:
                    //
                    //   trigger (all five params identical but for the reader)
                    //                         | elab pkg | golden DEF import copy
                    //   package typedef-dim select  PSEL |   flat   |   FLAT
                    //   package function-body select PFUN|   flat   |   FLAT
                    //   `p1::PSCP` whole read, module    |   flat   |  TAGGED
                    //   `PMOD.a` select, module          |  tagged  |  TAGGED
                    //   never read                  PNON |  tagged  |  TAGGED
                    //
                    // Neither the IMPORT KIND nor the HOLDER decides anything:
                    // arm `g4` puts the same parameter behind a wildcard HEADER
                    // import, a wildcard BODY import and an explicit
                    // `import p4::B4` and golden flattens all three (a module
                    // that only writes `p4::v4_t` and never imports has no copy
                    // at all, on both sides); arm `g3` materializes it into an
                    // interface, two module instances, a module with a plain
                    // parameter port list and a module whose port list selects
                    // it, and golden flattens every one.
                    //
                    // THE FENCES ARE INHERITED, NOT RE-DERIVED — every shape
                    // pass 48 fenced at the package face is fenced here, and arm
                    // `g5` (one package, one dead package-function select per
                    // parameter, only the SHAPE varying) shows golden's DEF
                    // import copy answering exactly as its elab package listing:
                    //   plain named pattern        -> FLAT   (the control)
                    //   pattern-valued element     -> TAGGED (patParamPatElem26v)
                    //   POSITIONAL pattern         -> untyped, operands resized
                    //   `real` member              -> TAGGED
                    //   `time` member              -> TAGGED
                    // and arm `g6` the class fence (`p6::CCLS`, selected only in
                    // a class method: golden TAGGED in both listings) which
                    // rides along for free because `hasMemberSelectOf` already
                    // returns at a `ClassDeclaration`.  The TYPE-KEY face is NOT
                    // MEASURABLE at this face at all: arm `g8` shows Surelog
                    // v1.87 raising `[ERR:UH0713] Unmatched member during
                    // pattern assignment` the moment a type-key pattern is
                    // MATERIALIZED into an importing module, where r11u49's `b3`
                    // reads the same parameter from a NON-importing module and
                    // compiles clean — an arm whose compile fails teaches error
                    // recovery, not golden, so the type/index-setter fence stays
                    // and is never widened.
                    //
                    // The >64-bit WIDTH rule does NOT apply here, and that is
                    // measured, not assumed: arm `g7`'s 96-bit packed `p7::WSEL`
                    // is package-selected and golden flattens its DEF import
                    // copy at 32/32/32, while its never-read sibling `p7::WNON`
                    // stays tagged — the member select outranks the width at
                    // this face exactly as it does in the package listing.
                    //
                    // Scope: the PHYSICAL definition view of a MODULE or
                    // INTERFACE only (`defViewOrig9 && !inPackage`).  A
                    // re-exporting PACKAGE's copy already flattens on the
                    // pristine binary (`g3`'s `p3b::B3`) and is left alone; the
                    // elaborated views are reached by their own road and are
                    // untouched.  `hasMemberSelectOf`'s OWN SET is unchanged
                    // (pass 48 keeps its bare-name predicate) but THIS face no
                    // longer reads it — the next paragraph says why.
                    // § importCopyDeclPkgSelect60v (r11v60) — THE TRIGGER IS
                    // RESOLVED TO THE DECLARING PACKAGE, never to a bare name.
                    // `hasMemberSelectOf` answers for ANY package (the r11u49
                    // R1 warning), and at THIS face that warning is not
                    // theoretical: verifier arm `v1.sv` puts an identically
                    // named struct localparam in two packages, selects only
                    // `pa`'s, and golden keeps `work@v1q.HIT` (the copy of
                    // `pb::HIT`) TAGGED while the bare-name test flattens it.
                    // Arm `v7.sv` is the second face: an UNQUALIFIED select of
                    // an IMPORT COPY inside another package (`CFGY.a` written
                    // in `pd`) reduces THAT copy — golden flattens
                    // `work@v7d.CFGY` and keeps `work@v7q.CFGY` (the declaring
                    // package `pc`'s own copy) TAGGED — while a SCOPED
                    // `pc::CFGX.a` written in `pd` reduces `pc`'s own
                    // `param_assign` and golden flattens `work@v7q.CFGX`.  So
                    // the predicate is "a select resolving to THIS package's
                    // param_assign, written in some package scope": unqualified
                    // inside the declaring package, or spelled `pkg::NAME.` in
                    // any package.  Both the corpus witnesses satisfy it —
                    // `iface_zero_width_field`'s `p::B` is selected in `p`, and
                    // BlackBePipeInt's `bp_default_cfg_p` / `bp_unicore_cfg_p`
                    // are declared AND selected inside `bp_common_pkg`.
                    const Symbol* homePkg60 = nullptr;
                    for (auto* hs60 = ps.getParentScope(); hs60;) {
                        auto& hsym60 = hs60->asSymbol();
                        if (hsym60.kind == SymbolKind::Package) {
                            homePkg60 = &hsym60;
                            break;
                        }
                        hs60 = hsym60.getParentScope();
                    }
                    if (!inPackage && isImported && defViewOrig9 && !funcScope &&
                        (pctS.kind == SymbolKind::PackedStructType ||
                         pctS.kind == SymbolKind::UnpackedStructType) &&
                        k0 == ExpressionKind::StructuredAssignmentPattern &&
                        upe49 &&
                        upe49->kind ==
                            ExpressionKind::StructuredAssignmentPattern &&
                        !patParamPatElem26v(*upe49) &&
                        !structHasSymbolicMemberDim(pctS) && homePkg60 &&
                        hasMemberSelectOfInPkg(homePkg60->name, ps.name)) {
                        auto& sapI60 =
                            upe49->as<StructuredAssignmentPatternExpression>();
                        bool ok60 = sapI60.typeSetters.empty() &&
                                    sapI60.indexSetters.empty();
                        // § packedTimeMemberDeclined60v (r11v60) — the
                        // non-integral / `time` fence runs over BOTH packings.
                        // r11u49 scoped it to the unpacked struct because "a
                        // packed struct's fields are integral by construction",
                        // which is true and beside the point: `time` IS
                        // integral, it is legal in a packed struct, and golden
                        // declines it there too.  Verifier arm `v2.sv` —
                        // `struct packed { time t; logic [31:0] a; }`,
                        // package-selected, imported: golden keeps
                        // `work@v2q.TPK` TAGGED and flattens its plain packed
                        // sibling `work@v2q.CPK`.  Without this the law
                        // flattened TPK with the `time` operand still at 64.
                        if (ok60)
                            for (auto& fld60 :
                                 (pctS.kind == SymbolKind::PackedStructType
                                      ? (const Scope&)
                                            pctS.as<PackedStructType>()
                                      : (const Scope&)
                                            pctS.as<UnpackedStructType>())
                                     .membersOfType<FieldSymbol>()) {
                                auto& ft60 =
                                    fld60.getType().getCanonicalType();
                                if (!ft60.isIntegral() ||
                                    (ft60.kind ==
                                         SymbolKind::PredefinedIntegerType &&
                                     ft60.as<PredefinedIntegerType>()
                                             .integerKind ==
                                         PredefinedIntegerType::Time)) {
                                    ok60 = false;
                                    break;
                                }
                            }
                        impPkgSelFlat60 = ok60;
                    }
                    // § elabUnpStructOperandWidth61 (r11u61) — EVERY OPERAND OF
                    // A FLAT ELABORATED STRUCT PATTERN IS SIZED AT ITS MEMBER'S
                    // WIDTH, NOT ONLY THE FIRST.
                    //
                    // THE PRODUCER, read in the pinned Surelog v1.87.  An
                    // instance's parameter whose value is an assignment pattern
                    // is DEEP-CLONED per instance and then reduced —
                    // `NetlistElaboration.cpp:262-283`:
                    //     param_assign* pclone = clone_tree(mod_assign, ...);
                    //     if (substituteAssignedValue(rhs, ...))
                    //       rhs = expandPatternAssignment(ts, rhs, mod, ..., instance);
                    // `substituteAssignedValue`
                    // (`CompileExpression.cpp:83-105`) answers the command-line
                    // `parametersubstitution` flag for a pattern op, so the
                    // reduce runs on EVERY elaborated copy — no member-select,
                    // scoped-reference or import predicate is involved at this
                    // face at all.  `expandPatternAssignment`
                    // (`CompileHelper.cpp:5207`) first FLATTENS
                    // (`ExprEval::flattenPatternAssignments`, which strips the
                    // `tagged_pattern` wrappers), and its UNTAGGED arm
                    // (`CompileHelper.cpp:5409-5441`) then walks the operands:
                    //     for (any* op : *operands) {
                    //       expr* vexp = reduceExpr(cop, invalidValue, ...);
                    //       uint64_t v = eval.get_uvalue(invalidValue, vexp);
                    //       int32_t csize = adjustOpSize(tps, cop, opIndex, ...);
                    //       if (invalidValue) { return result; }
                    //       ... if (shift < 0 || shift >= 64) return result; ...
                    //       opIndex++;
                    //     }
                    // and `adjustOpSize` (`CompileHelper.cpp:5114-5158`) matches
                    // `index == opIndex` against the struct typespec's MEMBERS
                    // and writes `cop->VpiSize(Bits(member))`.  So the rule is
                    // "size operand i at member i's width", for EVERY i up to
                    // and INCLUDING the first operand that does not reduce.
                    //
                    // The committed "a re-emitted OUTERMOST pattern sizes its
                    // FIRST operand" stamp (`ExprEmit.cpp`, the `reemitting`
                    // arm) is the SPECIAL CASE of this walk where operand 0 is a
                    // `hier_path` that never reduces: `adjustOpSize` runs before
                    // the `invalidValue` bail, so exactly one operand is sized.
                    // It stays untouched here — this law does not widen it.
                    //
                    // WHY ONLY THE UNPACKED STRUCT.  The PACKED struct's
                    // elaborated copy already reaches the flat op75 walk through
                    // `structPat`, which sizes every operand; grid arm `h2`
                    // (four packed members at 4 / 12 / 33 / 6 bits x plain /
                    // based / enum / concat / replication / default / positional
                    // values) is byte-identical to golden on the pristine
                    // binary, every cell.  The UNPACKED twin `h3` (the same
                    // sweep over `byte` / `shortint` / `int` / `longint`) is
                    // wrong in EVERY cell: golden folds and sizes
                    // `8 16 32 64` throughout while the pristine binary leaves
                    // `8 64 64 64`, keeps a concat as `op33` and a replication
                    // as `op34`, and keeps a based literal at its source width.
                    //
                    // WHAT DECIDES NOTHING, measured.  Arm `h6` carries one
                    // unpacked struct through an imported package parameter, a
                    // module localparam, an interface localparam, a parameter
                    // PORT, an OVERRIDDEN parameter port and two generate-block
                    // localparams: golden sizes every operand of every one, so
                    // the holder, the import, the override and the copy count
                    // are all irrelevant — it is the ELABORATED VIEW alone.
                    //
                    // THE FENCES ARE THE BAIL, and arm `h5` measures each one
                    // with UNBASED literals so "sized at the member width" can
                    // never be confused with "kept its own":
                    //   member kinds (byte, X, shortint) x the value `2`
                    //     X = shortint (the control) -> golden 8 / 16 / 16
                    //     X = logic [95:0]           -> golden 8 / 96 / 64
                    //         (sized, then the per-bit loop's `shift >= 64`
                    //          bails and the THIRD operand keeps 64)
                    //     X = time                   -> golden 8 / 64 / 64
                    //     X = string                 -> golden 8 /  8 / 64
                    //     X = real                   -> golden 8 / 32 / 16
                    //         (NO bail — but `Bits(real)` is 32, not the 64 a
                    //          `getBitWidth()` walk would write, so `real` is
                    //          declined rather than modelled wrong)
                    // and arm `h4` adds the two nested shapes — a PATTERN-VALUED
                    // member and an UNPACKED-ARRAY member — where golden keeps
                    // the inner `op75` as the operand and the walk bails on it,
                    // which the pristine binary ALREADY matches; both are
                    // declined so those right cells are never re-emitted.
                    //
                    // TWO MORE FENCES, and the ENCODING-VISIBLE grid is what
                    // found them (a lister that prints only `vpiSize` cannot
                    // see either):
                    //   * A CONCATENATION or REPLICATION member value.  Golden
                    //     keeps the reduce's RAW digit string and only rewrites
                    //     `vpiSize` — `adjustOpSize` calls `adjustUnsized`,
                    //     which re-encodes an UNSIZED constant only, then
                    //     `cop->VpiSize(ncsize)` — so a 12-bit member holding
                    //     `{4'b1010, 4'b0101}` is `BIN:10100101` AT SIZE 12,
                    //     eight digits in a twelve-bit constant.  This walk's
                    //     concat / replication arms write the ZERO-PADDED
                    //     string (`BIN:000010100101`).  That mismatch is
                    //     PRE-EXISTING on the packed road — grid `h2`'s `WCAT`
                    //     and `WREP` carry it identically on the pristine and
                    //     the law binary — so it is a residue of the walk, not
                    //     of this law, and the unpacked road is fenced off it
                    //     rather than joining it (`h3`'s `UCAT` / `UREP` keep
                    //     the pristine `op33` / `op34` operands).
